  "result": "I fixed it: integer ranges with an excluded bound now clamp to the nearest value inside the range. Previously they clamped to a value just outside it.\n\n**Cause:** In `IntRange._clamp` (`src/click/types.py:754`), the step off an excluded bound went the wrong way. I changed `return bound - dir` to `return bound + dir`. For example, with a minimum of 0 that excludes 0, an input of `-5` now clamps to `1` instead of `-1`. With a maximum of 10 that excludes 10, `15` now clamps to `9` instead of `11`. Ranges whose bounds are included were never affected.\n\n`FloatRange` doesn't need a change: it already refuses to clamp when a bound is excluded.\n\n**Tests:** I added six cases to `test_range` in `tests/test_types.py`. They cover an excluded minimum, an excluded maximum, and both together, including inputs exactly on the bound. `pytest tests/test_types.py tests/test_info_dict.py` gives 75 passed and 2 skipped. I didn't run the new cases against the old code to watch them fail; working through them by hand, the old line produces the wrong values they check for.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
